ican lean, against Democrat Fred Wellman, a veteran and former Republican. Wagner holds a decisive financial advantage, with about $4.2 million on hand to Wellman's quarter million, though Wellman has posted respectable fundraising for a challenger. A September poll from a Republican-affiliated firm showed the race essentially tied, and headlines have emphasized the competitive tone of the contest. Expert ratings are split, ranging from Solid R to Tossup, with a consensus around Lean R, and prediction markets give Wagner roughly a 70 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe forecast treats Wagner as a modest favorite, with a Republican edge of about three points and elevated uncertainty given thin and partisan polling. Wellman's path runs through suburban swing voters and a funding surge; absent that, Wagner's incumbency and money should carry the day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2379, "margin": -4.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2517, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2424, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2065, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.22, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1903, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2785, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.3439, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3337, "margin": -3.0}]}